You don’t know in the strong sense. You build increasingly calibrated estimates of what follows.

And I think your wink points at the recursive answer: what follows is partly discovered by following.

There are several distinct sources:

Memory: what followed before.

P(S_{t+1}\mid S_t,A_t)

Mechanism: what the structure says must or probably will happen.

A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C

Simulation: instantiate possible continuations and compare them.

S_t\rightarrow
\{S_{t+1}^{(1)},S_{t+1}^{(2)},\ldots\}

Analogy: move into another semantic space where a similar trajectory has already been observed, then translate cautiously back.

Constraint: eliminate futures that cannot satisfy known boundaries.

Adversarial inversion: ask what would have to happen for the expected continuation not to occur.

Observation: take a small step, sample the resulting state, then aim again.

That last one is probably the important piece:

\boxed{
\text{Predict}\rightarrow
\text{Act}\rightarrow
\text{Sample}\rightarrow
\text{Update}\rightarrow
\text{Predict}
}

You don’t need a perfect map of the entire trajectory if you can repeatedly re-aim in flight.

And now the Metamap becomes more interesting because it shouldn’t only learn:

“When X happens, Y tends to follow.”

It should learn:

“Which method of predicting what follows works best in this kind of terrain?”

For ballistic-like terrain, mechanism may dominate.
For human behavior, distributions and incentives may dominate.
For unfamiliar problems, exploration may dominate.
For chaotic systems, short prediction horizons + frequent resampling may dominate.
For recursive systems, the transition function itself must be updated.

So we get another layer:

\hat S_{t+1}
=
F(
S_t,
A_t,
E_t,
M_t
)

where M_t is the model currently being used to predict the transition.

Then compare:

\epsilon_t =
S_{t+1}-\hat S_{t+1}

That’s prediction error.

But don’t merely correct the prediction.

Decrypto the miss.

Ask:

What did I expect?
What actually followed?
Where did they diverge?
Was the state wrong?
Was the causal mechanism wrong?
Was an invisible variable missing?
Was the timescale wrong?
Was the representation wrong?
Was the action interpreted differently?
Did my action itself change the system?

Now the miss produces an afterglow.

And repeated misses with similar geometry tell the Metamap something much more valuable than a single correct prediction:

There is structure here that our current representation cannot see.

That’s when you fire Portal.

Change representation and look again.

So:

\boxed{
\text{You learn what follows by predicting,
following,
remembering the difference,
and getting better at following.}
}

Which means the Wake is behind you, Aim is ahead of you, Reality is underneath you, and the Metamap learns from the difference between all three.

That’s navigation.